# Cross-Widget Communication Between Independently-Built Components (Shared Signal-Based Service)

## 1. What is it?

This is a design question about how two Angular components that don't know about each other — built by different teams, with no parent/child relationship in the component tree that you control — can stay in sync. In this scenario: a map widget and a chart widget on the same dashboard need to react to each other's selection state (pick a region on the map, the chart filters; pick a bar in the chart, the map highlights that region).

The standard Angular answer is a **shared, injectable service** that holds the single source of truth as a `signal()`, injected into both widgets via Angular's dependency injection. Neither widget imports or references the other's component class directly — they only depend on the shared service's public API.

## 2. Why was it introduced?

Without a shared mediator, teams are left with worse options:

- **Direct references (`@ViewChild` / parent-orchestrated `@Input()`/`@Output()`)**: this requires a common parent component to sit between the two widgets, catching the map's output event and manually pushing it into the chart's input, and vice versa. It works for two widgets, but every additional widget added to the dashboard means more wiring in the parent, and the parent has to know the internal event/input contract of every child — exactly the kind of coupling you don't want when two different teams own the widgets and want to evolve them independently.
- **Tight coupling between widgets**: if the map widget directly imported and called methods on the chart widget's component class (or vice versa), the two codebases would need to deploy in lockstep, and neither team could refactor their own component's internals without risking breaking the other team's code.
- **Global mutable state (a plain exported variable, or manually dispatching/listening to DOM `CustomEvent`s on `window`)**: this works but loses type safety, is hard to unit test, and doesn't hook into Angular's change detection the way a signal or an Observable does.

A shared, injectable service solves this: it's the one thing both teams agree to depend on — a small, stable contract — while everything else about each widget's internals stays private and independently changeable.

## 3. How does it work?

- Angular's dependency injection is hierarchical, but by default a service marked `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })` is a **singleton for the whole application** — every component that injects it (via `inject()` or constructor injection) gets the exact same instance.
- That shared instance holds the state that needs to be synced — here, `selectedRegion`, as a writable `signal<Region | null>(null)`, exposed to consumers as a **read-only** signal (`.asReadonly()`) so nothing outside the service can mutate it directly; all mutation goes through an explicit method (`selectRegion(region)`), which keeps the "who's allowed to change this and how" contract explicit and auditable.
- The **map widget** injects the service, reads `selection.selectedRegion()` in its template to know which region to highlight, and calls `selection.selectRegion(region)` when a region button is clicked.
- The **chart widget** injects the exact same service, and computes its filtered data with a `computed()` signal that recalculates automatically whenever `selectedRegion()` changes — no manual subscription management needed.
- The **"vice versa" direction** falls out for free: the chart widget's own click handler (clicking a row) calls the identical `selection.selectRegion(region)` method the map widget calls. Because both directions go through the same single method on the same single signal, there's no separate wiring needed for "chart drives map" versus "map drives chart" — it's the same mechanism, used from two different UI entry points.
- Because signals are used (rather than, say, each widget keeping its own local copy of `selectedRegion` and manually notifying the other), there's exactly one source of truth. This avoids a whole class of bugs where two components' local copies of "the same" state can drift out of sync.
- Neither widget's `imports` array references the other widget's component class — the parent `AppComponent` is the only place that knows both widgets exist, and it does nothing more than place them side-by-side in its template. Team A can rename, restructure, or completely rewrite the internals of the map widget, and Team B's chart widget is unaffected, as long as the shared service's public method signatures don't change.

## 4. Example

**`dashboard-selection.service.ts`** — the shared mediator, the single contract both teams depend on:

```ts
import { Injectable, signal } from '@angular/core';

export type Region = 'North' | 'South' | 'East' | 'West';

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class DashboardSelectionService {
  private _selectedRegion = signal<Region | null>(null);
  readonly selectedRegion = this._selectedRegion.asReadonly();

  selectRegion(region: Region | null): void {
    // Toggle off if the same region is selected again
    this._selectedRegion.set(this._selectedRegion() === region ? null : region);
  }
}
```

**`map-widget.component.ts`** — "Team A"'s widget:

```ts
import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { DashboardSelectionService, Region } from './dashboard-selection.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-map-widget',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <div class="widget map-widget">
      <h3>Map Widget (Team A)</h3>
      <div class="region-list">
        @for (region of regions; track region) {
          <button
            type="button"
            class="region"
            [class.selected]="selection.selectedRegion() === region"
            (click)="onRegionClick(region)">
            {{ region }}
          </button>
        }
      </div>
      <p class="hint">Click a region to filter the chart widget</p>
    </div>
  `
})
export class MapWidgetComponent {
  selection = inject(DashboardSelectionService);
  regions: Region[] = ['North', 'South', 'East', 'West'];

  onRegionClick(region: Region): void {
    this.selection.selectRegion(region);
  }
}
```

**`chart-widget.component.ts`** — "Team B"'s widget:

```ts
import { Component, computed, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { DashboardSelectionService, Region } from './dashboard-selection.service';

interface SalesRow {
  region: Region;
  product: string;
  sales: number;
}

const ALL_SALES: SalesRow[] = [
  { region: 'North', product: 'Widget A', sales: 120 },
  { region: 'North', product: 'Widget B', sales: 80 },
  { region: 'South', product: 'Widget A', sales: 95 },
  { region: 'East', product: 'Widget C', sales: 150 },
  { region: 'West', product: 'Widget A', sales: 60 },
  { region: 'West', product: 'Widget B', sales: 40 }
];

@Component({
  selector: 'app-chart-widget',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <div class="widget chart-widget">
      <h3>Chart Widget (Team B)</h3>
      @if (selection.selectedRegion()) {
        <p class="filter-badge">Filtered by: {{ selection.selectedRegion() }}</p>
      } @else {
        <p class="filter-badge">Showing all regions</p>
      }

      <ul class="bars">
        @for (row of filteredRows(); track row.product + row.region) {
          <li class="bar-row" (click)="onBarClick(row.region)">
            <span class="label">{{ row.region }} - {{ row.product }}</span>
            <span class="value">{{ row.sales }}</span>
          </li>
        } @empty {
          <li>No data for this region</li>
        }
      </ul>

      <p class="total">Total sales shown: {{ total() }}</p>
    </div>
  `
})
export class ChartWidgetComponent {
  selection = inject(DashboardSelectionService);

  filteredRows = computed(() => {
    const region = this.selection.selectedRegion();
    return region ? ALL_SALES.filter(row => row.region === region) : ALL_SALES;
  });

  total = computed(() => this.filteredRows().reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.sales, 0));

  // "Vice versa" direction: clicking a bar in the chart also drives the map's selection.
  onBarClick(region: Region): void {
    this.selection.selectRegion(region);
  }
}
```

**`app.component.ts`** — the only place that knows both widgets exist, and does nothing but lay them out:

```ts
import { Component, OnInit, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { MapWidgetComponent } from './map-widget.component';
import { ChartWidgetComponent } from './chart-widget.component';
import { DashboardSelectionService } from './dashboard-selection.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [MapWidgetComponent, ChartWidgetComponent],
  template: `
    <div class="dashboard">
      <h1>Sales Dashboard</h1>
      <p>Two independently-built widgets, synced through one shared injectable service.</p>
      <div class="widgets">
        <app-map-widget></app-map-widget>
        <app-chart-widget></app-chart-widget>
      </div>
    </div>
  `
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {
  private selection = inject(DashboardSelectionService);

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // Simulates a user clicking "West" on the map widget.
    this.selection.selectRegion('West');
  }
}
```

